From ddae4beb75d900e3e5209dd8f7e64f2e308b09f5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 11:27:10 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 40/43] uml: commit Darwin guest RAM on demand

Free-page reporting was written against a sparse physical-memory file,
punching reported ranges out of it. The Darwin runtime now backs guest
RAM with anonymous memory, has nothing to punch, and refuses the
request, so the reporter disabled itself on its first attempt and a
guest that once peaked kept that memory for the rest of the boot. On
physical iOS the whole of mem= was wired before the guest started, so
the footprint never moved at all.

Make the guest tell the host which RAM it uses. Guest RAM starts
uncommitted; mem_init() commits what memblock handed out before the page
allocator existed, and arch_alloc_page() commits a chunk the first time
the allocator hands out one of its pages, outside the zone lock and
before the caller can touch it. A host that wires guest RAM wires it
then. Free-page reporting releases free chunks again: the host drops
them from the process footprint and unwires them on iOS. The host keeps
a released range's contents only until it wants the memory and does not
count it, so it has to be committed again before reuse; skipping that
would let the emulator exceed its memory limit unseen and leave reused
guest pages unwired. One bit per 32-page chunk records what the host
holds, and the hook costs the allocation fast path one load once every
chunk is committed.

This adds darwin_uml_physmem_release() and darwin_uml_physmem_commit()
to the bridge, version 12, and retires the file hole-punch path.
